div.p:
Against the second conclusion it is argued thus: kings often command something that is repugnant to the divine law; 
therefore one is not obliged to these laws, otherwise one would be obligated to two opposing laws. 
Furthermore, it follows from this that men would sin if they did not give the king the complete tax, but rather defrauded him by hiding eight or nine barrels of wine from the one who receives the eighth part for the king. And thus, someone would sin if they did not leave a denarius in a house where a sign hangs representing that money, even if no one asked for it.
